1: //! win-1: a transfer session must fit a small stack.
2: //!
3: //! The CLI polls its whole command future on the process's MAIN thread
4: //! (`#[tokio::main]`), and Windows gives that thread 1 MiB. A debug
5: //! build's async poll frames hold every nested future inline, so when
6: //! the SOURCE_SIDE_CONTAINMENT work grew the destination routes, every
7: //! `blit` run that was a DESTINATION died on Windows with `thread 'main'
8: //! has overflowed its stack` — while macOS and Linux, whose main threads
9: //! get 8 MiB, passed. The fix boxes the session bodies where one large
10: //! future nests another (`transfers::local::run`, the local session's
11: //! role join, `run_source`/`run_destination`, the pull/push clients, the
12: //! retry loop), so a caller holds a pointer instead of the session.
13: //!
14: //! This guard runs the same shapes on every platform, identically: each
15: //! session is driven on a thread whose stack is exactly [`BUDGET`], with
16: //! a current-thread runtime (the future is polled on that thread, as
17: //! `block_on` polls the CLI's). Measured minimums of this thread's stack
18: //! (DEVLOG 2026-09-30, "win-1"), macOS aarch64 debug: before the boxing
19: //! the local mirror needed 368 KiB, the pull-shaped destination 208 KiB
20: //! in-stream and 192 KiB on the data plane; after it 160 / 160 / 144 KiB
21: //! (x86_64 debug: 176 / 176 / 160 KiB). So at this layer only the local
22: //! mirror goes red before the boxing: 1 MiB reproduces nothing here, and
23: //! the pull regression lived in the CLI's own frames —
24: //! `crates/blit-cli/tests/main_thread_stack_budget.rs` holds the real
25: //! binary to its budget and is red before the fix on every shape,
26: //! pull included. This file keeps the sessions themselves small on every
27: //! platform.
28: //!
29: //! The pull shape's SOURCE runs on its own thread and runtime: in a real
30: //! pull it is the daemon, and only the DESTINATION is on the caller's
31: //! stack.
